It's not really pertinent.  Once you start developing a fork, it's
useful to have a seperate name to distinguish it--it's hardly
conceivable that a new version of Rogue is coming down the pipeline,
yet even if the name were legally available it wouldn't make sense to
appropriate it for Nethack 4.0.  If people wanted to develop a
community fork, there's no good reason not to rename it to OpenHack or
whatever, especially when there's good evidence (bug reports being
closed, etc) that the DevTeam is actively doing some work on the
codebase and having 2 things named Nethack out there would simply be
confusing.  And, of course, the nethack.org domain is already taken.

In short, there's a good reason for the convention of renaming forks,
and it's exactly the same reason that we as humans try to assign some
sort of unique name to any two different things we need to distinguish.



